      "id": 1,
      "text": "Charles Whittlesey named the Marengo River in 1849 to commemorate the myriads of mosquitoes inhabiting its banks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marengo%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Bad River watershed in Marengo was originally part of the traditional territory of the Lake Superior Band of Ojibwe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marengo_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Logging became the dominant economic driver in Marengo by the late 1800s, focusing on white pine and hemlock forests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marengo_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Chequamegon National Forest helped stabilize rural development in Marengo through reforestation and fire suppression programs starting in 1933.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marengo_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Brunsweiler River flows south to north through Marengo as a tributary of the Marengo River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marengo%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Marengo is located near the North Country Trail, which stretches all the way from New York to North Dakota.",
